  (20155,'pc-l16',1,'grammar','Waray and diri negators w/ ma- Actor Focus','In this lesson you will learn how to negate a ma-actor focus sentence using the negators WARAY and DIRI. These are two of the most confusing words for non-native Waray speakers. However, each is to be used with a specific form. In ma-actor focus sentences, both waray and diri mean NO or NOT.

WARAY is used when referring to PAST or PRESENT action.
DIRI is used when referring to FUTURE action.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20156,'pc-l16',2,'vocab',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20157,'pc-l16',3,'drill',null,null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20158,'pc-l16',4,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,'If action already begun (past or present) is referred to, USE WARAY!
If action not yet begun (future) is referred to, USE DIRI!
Often, a more complete answer is given such as:

| Negator  | Context                | Meaning     |
| :------- | :--------------------- | :---------- |
| waray pa | w/ past/present action | "not yet"   |
| waray na | w/ past/present action | "not now"   |
| diri pa  | w/ future action       | "not yet"   |
| diri na  | w/ future action       | "not now"   |

These, most often, follow a question that uses na or pa. However, they are also used in sentences without na or pa to show the various meanings below:

| Negator  | Meaning (nuance)                               |
| :------- | :--------------------------------------------- |
| waray pa | "not yet" (but plan to do it)                  |
| waray na | "not now" or "no more" (and no plan to do it) |
| diri pa  | "not yet" (but plan to do it)                  |
| diri na  | "not now" or "no more" (and no plan to do it) |

[NOTE: Sometimes expressions like waray na or diri na should not be taken too seriously. For example, if a Filipino comes to your house at supper time, and you say, "Makaon na ''ta," he may reply, "Diri na la!" This is only showing politeness or shame. If you ask him again, he may agree.]'),
  (20159,'pc-l16',5,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20160,'pc-l16',6,'drill',null,null,null,'production','voice','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20161,'pc-l16',7,'drill',null,null,null,'production','voice','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20162,'pc-l16',8,'drill',null,null,null,'production','voice','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20163,'pc-l16',9,'drill',null,null,null,'production','type','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20164,'pc-l16',10,'assessment','Checkpoint — pass to continue',null,null,'production','type','none','both',0.8,true,null),
  (20165,'pc-l17',1,'grammar','Negating ma-Actor Focus Sentences with waray and diri','In the previous lesson you learned when to use the negators waray and diri for a short response to a question. In this lesson you will learn the long form response using waray and diri.

In English short answers (like no or yes) are sometimes given to questions; at other times, a longer negative or positive answer is needed.

For example:
Q: "Are you going to town today?"
A: "No." [short answer]
A: "No, I''m not going to town today." [long answer]

In Waray, the same is true. You may also answer with a short form or a long form answer.

For example:
Q: "Makadto ka ba ha siyudad?"
A: "Diri." [short form]
A: "Diri. Diri ko makadto ha siyudad."',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20166,'pc-l17',2,'grammar','waray/diri with I Class Personal Pronouns','If the subject is a I Class Personal Pronoun (ako, ikaw, hiya, kita, kami, kamo, hira), the pronoun comes after the waray/diri and before the verb. (except when there are other particles)','Waray + I Cl Pers Pro + Um - Verb or Ma- AFV + rest...
Diri + particles + I Cl. Pers. Pro. + Ma - Verb + rest',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20167,'pc-l17',3,'grammar','waray/diri with I Class General Pronoun or I Class Marker','If the subject of the sentence is marked by a I Class General Pronoun (ini, iton, adto), or a I Class Marker (hi, hira, an, an mga), the verb comes after the waray or diri (except when there are particles). The subject then follows the verb.','Waray + Um - Verb or Ma- AFV + I Cl Gen Pro/I Cl Marker + rest....
Diri + particles + Ma - Verb + I Cl. Gen. Pro./I Cl. Marker',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20168,'pc-l17',4,'grammar','Double Negator','In English, a double negator is often used to answer a question.

For example:
Q: Are you going to church?
A: No. I''m not going to church today.

The double negator is also often used in Waray. This adds emphasis and more explanation.

For example:
Q: Matan-aw ba kamo hin sine?
A: Diri. Diri kami matan-aw hin sine.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20169,'pc-l17',5,'vocab',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20170,'pc-l17',6,'drill',null,null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20171,'pc-l17',7,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20172,'pc-l17',8,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20173,'pc-l17',9,'drill',null,null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20174,'pc-l17',10,'drill',null,null,null,'production','type','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20175,'pc-l17',11,'assessment','Checkpoint — pass to continue',null,null,'production','type','none','both',0.8,true,null),
  (20176,'pc-l18',1,'grammar','Waray and diri negators with particles','One of the most difficult things for a non-native Waray speaker to learn is where to put the small particles in the sentence. This becomes even more difficult when the sentence is negated. Thus, in order to get all the particles in the sentence, the sentence sometimes comes out looking like this:

INCORRECT: Waray hira Jim hi ngan James makadto liwat pa ba?
CORRECT: Waray pa ba liwat makadto hira hi Jim ngan James?

If particles like pa, na, ba, liwat, are used in a negation sentence, the word order using waray and diri is the same as in lesson #17 with the exception that the particles must be placed in the formula.

If I Class Personal Pronouns are used, the pronoun precedes the verb (as before). However, the particle comes after waray/diri.

The particles that you have learned will usually be placed in a sentence in the following order:

| Particle Order           |
|:-------------------------|
| na ba                    |
| pa ba                    |
| ba liwat/gihapon         |
| na liwat/gihapon         |
| pa liwat/gihapon         |
| na ba liwat/gihapon      |
| pa ba liwat/gihapon      |','Waray/Diri + (ka) + I Class Personal Pronoun (except ka) + Ma-AFV + ...',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20177,'pc-l18',2,'grammar','Waray/Diri with I Class General Pronouns/or Markers','If I Class General Pronouns or I Class Markers are used, the verb precedes the general pronoun or marker (as before). The particle again comes after waray/diri.','Waray + PARTICLES + Um-Verb or Ma- AFV + I Cl Gen Pro/Marker + rest...
Diri + particles + Ma - Verb + I Cl. Gen Pro/Marker + rest',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20178,'pc-l18',3,'vocab',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20179,'pc-l18',4,'drill',null,null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20180,'pc-l18',5,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,'Remember that ka is a VIP word that comes before the particles.
These sentences may be translated more than one way. The meaning will be determined by the context and the inflection of the voice.'),
  (20181,'pc-l18',6,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20182,'pc-l18',7,'drill',null,null,null,'production','voice','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20183,'pc-l18',8,'drill',null,null,null,'production','voice','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20184,'pc-l18',9,'drill',null,null,null,'production','type','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20185,'pc-l18',10,'assessment','Checkpoint — pass to continue',null,null,'production','type','none','both',0.8,true,null),
  (20186,'pc-l19',1,'grammar','Mag- Actor Focus','In the previous lessons you learned how to use Ma- Actor Focus. There is another actor focus verb which is called Mag- Actor Focus. You will be happy to know that this focus is used the same way as Ma- Actor Focus. So it will be very easy to learn now.

Like in Ma-Actor Focus, the TOPIC of the Mag- Actor Focus sentence is the actor, or the doer of the action (the subject of the verb).

The following chart shows the verb root, the past/present form, the future form, and the command form of some common mag-actor focus verbs.

| Root | Past Tense | Present Tense | Future tense | Command |
| :--- | :--------- | :------------ | :----------- | :------ |
| | nag + root | nag + reduplication of the 1st syllable | mag + root | pag + root |
| -dara | nagdara | nagdadara | magdara | pagdara |
| -bantay | nagbantay | nagbabantay | magbantay | pagbantay |
| -trabaho | nagtrabaho | nagtratrabaho | magtrabaho | pagtrabaho |
| -aram | nag-aram | nag-aaram | mag-aaram | pag-aram |
| -mando | nagmando | nagmamando | magmando | pagmando |
| -ukoy | nag-ukoy | nag-uukoy | mag-ukoy | pag-ukoy |
| -asawa | nag-asawa | nag-aasawa | mag-asawa | pag-asawa |
| -andam | nag-andam | nag-aandam | mag-andam | pag-andam |
| -siring | nagsiring | nagsisiring | magsiring | pagsiring |
| -hulat | naghulat | naghuhulat | maghulat | paghulat |
| -lirong | naglirong | naglilirong | maglirong | paglirong |
| -kinahanglan | nagkinahanglan | nagkikinahanglan | magkinahanglan | pagkinahanglan |

Some verbs in Waray usually use the mag- prefix, while other verbs usually use the ma- prefix. However, some verbs use either of the two prefixes depending on the intended meaning. The ma- verbs usually intend to show action that is of short duration. Many times, these are related to motion or movement. For example, matindog, malingkod, madagan, or malakat. The mag- verbs, on the other hand, usually intend to show action that is being done over a longer period of time.','Mag- Verb + I CI Actor + II CI Object + III CI L/D/B',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20187,'pc-l19',2,'grammar','Verbalizing Nouns','Filipinos are fond of verbalizing nouns with nag-/mag- prefixes. That is, a noun is made into a verb by prefixing it with nag-, mag-, or pag-. English nouns are often also verbalized, especially when there is no common Waray word. Verbalized nouns are often used as a short-cut. For example: (verbalized nouns)',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20188,'pc-l19',3,'vocab',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20189,'pc-l19',4,'drill',null,null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20190,'pc-l19',5,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20191,'pc-l19',6,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,'pakadto means "going to, or going in the direction of" [The verb in these verbalized nouns is understood.]'),
  (20192,'pc-l19',7,'drill',null,null,null,'production','voice','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20193,'pc-l19',8,'drill',null,null,null,'production','type','none','both',null,null,null),
  (20194,'pc-l20',1,'grammar','Nga linker','The nga linker is used to join modifiers to a noun. Note: The non-contracted form nga is used when the preceding word ends in a consonant and vowel.

*   hataas nga babaye
*   ini nga babaye
*   aslum nga prutas
*   maduroto nga lalake',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20195,'pc-l20',2,'grammar','Particles','One of the most difficult things you learned in these lessons was the word order of the particles. Perhaps the following summary chart will help you to remember which of these comes first.

**WORD ORDER OF PARTICLES**','na/pa + ba + liwat/gihapon + hin duro',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20196,'pc-l20',3,'grammar','Hin duro','The intensifier hin duro, which means "very, so, extremely", follows the adjective it modifies... ...but comes after other particles ...and can come before or after a I Class Personal Pronoun.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20197,'pc-l20',4,'grammar','Waray and Diri Negators','There are a few things you need to remember about negating with waray and diri.

1.  Waray is used when referring to PAST or PRESENT action.
    Diri is used when referring to FUTURE action.
2.  Both waray and diri come first in the sentence.
3.  Waray will take Um verb and the future form of the verb in Ma-Verb.
4.  Diri will take the future form of the verb.
5.  The usual word order is:
    `Waray + particles + I Cl. Per. Pron. + Um & Ma-Verb`
    `Diri + particles + I CI. Per. Pron. + FUTURE Verb`

    except with I Class Markers/General Pronouns
    `Waray/Diri + particles + Verb + I CI. Markers/ gen. Pronouns`

    and with particles and the VIP ka:
    `Wala/Dili + ka + particles + ...`

b) If the particles na and pa are used with the negators, the meaning is:

*   Waray pa/Diri pa -- "not yet, still not" (but plan to do it)
*   Waray na/Diri na -- "not now, no more" (and no plan to do it)

14) The double negator is often used to answer a question negatively. It adds emphasis and explanation.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20198,'pc-l20',5,'grammar','Mag - Actor Focus','The same in Ma-actor Focus but this has the Command Form of the Verb. Remember the formula.','Mag-Verb + I Cl. Actor + II Cl. Obj. + III Cl. Loc/Dir/Ben',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20199,'pc-l20',6,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,'The personal pronoun KA is a VIP pronoun and comes before all of the particles.'),
