  (20220,'pc-l22',1,'grammar','Special verbs','Karuyag, Buot, Kinahanglan, Pwede, Mahimo
These are called special verbs, or pseudo-verbs. These special verbs may function as verbs themselves.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20221,'pc-l22',2,'grammar',null,'However, these special verbs are most often linked to other verbs in a sentence as an "auxiliary" to the main verb of the sentence. Used in this way, these special verbs have the following meanings:

| Special Verbs | Meaning |
|---|---|
| KARUYAG/BUOT | "like to, want to, or wish to (do something)" |
| KINAHANGLAN | "need to, have to, must (do something)" |
| PWEDE/MAHIMO | "can, may, able to, it is possible to (do something)" |

In the following examples you will notice that these special verbs are linked to another main verb of the sentence',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20222,'pc-l22',3,'grammar','Word Order','The FORMULAS below will show you the word order for sentences using special verbs. There are two common ways of arranging the words -- the subject before the main verb, or the subject after the main verb. Either way is acceptable though Waray often prefer one or the other.

Examples will be given using both formulas, or word orders above. Both are used depending upon location and personal preference.','SUBJECT BEFORE MAIN VERB: Special Verb + I Cl Subject + Future Verb (no reduplication of the 1st syll. of the root) + rest... (EXCEPTION w/ Kinahanglan, Pwede, Mahimo: Special Verb + I Cl Marker + Future Verb (no reduplication) + rest...); SUBJECT AFTER MAIN VERB: Special Verb + Future Verb (no reduplication) + I Cl Subject + rest...',null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20223,'pc-l22',4,'grammar','KARUYAG / BUOT','"like to, want to, wish to, will to (do something)"
Karuyag and buot mean the same thing and may be used in the same way. The more common usage is karuyag, but buot is also used.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20224,'pc-l22',5,'grammar','KINAHANGLAN','"need to, have to, must (do something)"',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20225,'pc-l22',6,'grammar','PWEDE/MAHIMO','"can, may, able to, it is possible to (do something)"
Pwede and mahimo mean the same thing, and may be used in the same way. Pwede is actually a Spanish word that is commonly used by Waray. It is the more common of these two in most places.',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20226,'pc-l22',7,'vocab',null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null),
  (20227,'pc-l22',8,'drill',null,null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20228,'pc-l22',9,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,null),
  (20229,'pc-l22',10,'drill','Examples',null,null,'recognition','mc','peek',null,null,null,'Notice in the examples above:
The special verbs are used with another main verb.
All the main verbs are future tense; there is no reduplication of the first syllable of the root.
The main verbs are translated in English like infinitives most of the time. (to buy, to play, to work, to go)
The sentences begin with the special verbs.
The subject sometimes comes before the main verb; at other times, it follows the main verb.'),
